1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
 In this lesson we're going to explore some of TypeScripts core types.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
 Now, undefined, any, unknown, and never.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
 Then by the end of this lesson you're going to know

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:19,000
 when to use each of these types in real-world scenarios.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 First of all, let's have a look at now.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 You may already be familiar with this when using JavaScript.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,000
 Let's go through a bit of an explanation just to make sure you're aware.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
 The null type represents an intentional absence of any value.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 So let's do an example.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:48,000
 Let's do let middle name of type string.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
 It could be string or it could be null.

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,000
 And we're going to default it to null.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
 So in this particular example, if we have a person and we have a middle name,

14
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
 they may not actually have a middle name.

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
 So we can actually default it to null.

16
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,000
 But we can then actually sign it to a value later on.

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 So of course we could do middle name equals, let's say, an, for example.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:27,000
 So again, the null is used here to indicate that we have some absence,

19
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 but we may actually want to assign it to a value later on.

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 Now let's have a look at undefinedness and comparison.

21
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:42,000
 Undefined type represents a variable that exists,

22
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 but it hasn't actually been initialized yet.

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:56,000
 So let's do some real-world example. Let's have a last login.

24
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 And this could be set to date or it could be undefined.

25
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 We do a console log at the moment of our last login date.

26
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 We're of course going to sit as undefined.

27
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:26,000
 But then later on, we could then log in and do last login equals new date.

28
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:33,000
 And if we then log that to the console, we're going to get our date.

29
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:41,000
 Now the difference between this and now is that undefined usually means that something is missing

30
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
 and it's going to be assigned later.

31
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
 So in this particular example, a user hasn't yet logged in,

32
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
 so we don't actually know their login time.

33
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 Whereas null, for example, we use that in the case of that middle name,

34
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 because they may never ever assign a value to that value.

35
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:15,000
 So the difference here is of course when someone's logged in, they might actually more likely going to log in at some point.

36
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 And that's why we can set it to undefined because we don't know it at the current stage,

37
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,000
 but it's more likely to be have a value later on.

38
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
 Whereas if we're null in a case of a middle name,

39
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 they may never have a middle name, which means it kind of makes a small sense to assign it to null.

40
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 And now let's have a look at any.

41
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
 So the any type allows a variable to hold, as the name suggests, any value.

42
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,000
 And this turns off TypeScript's type checking ability.

43
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:54,000
 This can be useful when we're working with any legacy code,

44
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
 or perhaps under type third party libraries.

45
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:07,000
 But we also need to be careful because over using the any type basically removes the safety net that TypeScript provides.

46
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
 So it's going to be used as it kind of last resort.

47
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
 So let's do it in quick example.

48
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:18,000
 Let's say we have a variable of data.

49
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 We're going to assign it to any type.

50
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,000
 And then as the name suggests, we can assign any values.

51
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,000
 We could say like 42.

52
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
 We could then say data equals hello.

53
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 Or maybe data equals true.

54
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
 So we can see that we are now just morphing the type from a number,

55
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:47,000
 or the way through to string to Boolean and TypeScript really doesn't care.

56
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:57,000
 So again, just use the any whenever you maybe you absolutely have to if you're working with some legacy code, for example.

57
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 But now be careful because of the ways there's no point doing TypeScript.

58
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
 And now let's have a look at Unknown.

59
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:18,000
 So we could have a same response of Unknown.

60
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
 And we can assign any values to it.

61
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
 We cannot use it directly without a type check.

62
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:33,000
 So this is perfect for doing things like dynamic input or perhaps passing API responses.

63
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:39,000
 When you need to validate the type before using the value.

64
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:43,000
 So imagine receiving data from an API.

65
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:48,000
 Now you don't actually know that type yet when you're receiving a data.

66
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:56,000
 So using Unknown forces us to check the type before we're doing some performing operations.

67
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
 So let's just do a quick example.

68
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:08,000
 So let's have a function and the function could be just a throw error.

69
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,000
 And we have a message.

70
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:23,000
 And we are going to throw the type is going to be never because we don't actually know what the type of that error is going to be at the point.

71
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:29,000
 But we can do throw error or throw new error.

72
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
 And just pass the message.

73
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:36,000
 So this is a very typical pattern for obviously during errors.

74
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
 Another example where we could have a function like an infinite loop.

75
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,000
 Not quite sure why you do that in practice, but here's how you could do it.

76
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,000
 So we have a function where it returned never.

77
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:55,000
 And then we just do while true and we just loop around the opposite infinitely.

78
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:03,000
 And because we're never going to return from this function, we can then assign the return type of never.

79
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:10,000
 Now let's look through a little bit of a better example of perhaps using a natural API.

80
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:13,000
 So you can see it's used.

81
